 does that sort of did that play any  havoc with your mind  when you're standing on set acting and  you're like oh  would i change that lens scott and i are  our buddies we've been friends  we met through the sundance labs and  when i was making my first movie the  diary of a teenage girl and so i have  visited  him on sets before and he's watched cuts  of my movies i've watched cuts of his  things we really have a  relationship director to director much  more than director to actor  um and so it was a very weird  shift for me to show up on set and to be  in actor mode and i really tried to put  my director hat  aside and say you know i'm here to do  one job and for me  it was glorious it was like i  got to as a director you have to think  about everything you are thinking about  not only how the scene is going to play  out how the acting is going to go what  lenses you're going to use how you're  going to shoot it but also the clouds  and are they going to move and are we  gonna make lunch  and what about tomorrow and do i need to  rewrite that scene and  you have more things on your plate than  i can possibly explain and as an actor  you get to come in and be  incredibly focused on one thing and you  have to do that well but you have to do  one thing and so for me it was great  because scott would say like  okay so i know this scene looks weird  we're gonna do this in vfx and i'd go  scott  not my problem i don't care i'm not  thinking about that i don't have to go  to your vfx meeting i don't have to look  at those proofs i'm good like it made me  i felt this lightness where i didn't  have the weight of  that responsibility on my shoulders but  he and steven meisner the  dp would tease me all the time i would  come in and they'd go so how would you  film this  oh well i guess mari could have shot  this better you know it was there was a  constant  or they'd look at me and go what do you  want to say give us the note  i would be like no i'm not backseat  directing i swear  that's great uh one of the things that i  really responded to  about the series is the fact that  there's no villain that  the mom is not some evil person that the  the other chess players are not out to  get her or steal  i mean it's just it's refreshing and the  villain is is anya trying to overcome  herself  yeah she's she's got her own struggles  with her own mind that she has to  overcome  and it's really in that way that's why  it is such a coming-of-age story and  it's about her relationship with herself  and what it is to be a woman i think too  you know part of what i loved about my  character being such a complex  not villain but not a perfect mother  figure in any way  and also you know beth's real biological  mother both of us are these sort of like  cautionary tales of who you don't want  to be i'm this alcoholic  mother who never got to fulfill her  dreams who's been locked in a loveless  marriage and then she has  her biological mother who pretty much  went crazy  and you just think like these are her  examples of womanhood these are who she  could become and she has to forge her  own path  and find a way to really be herself  and it takes her a long time but she  gets there you guys got to film in  berlin  and right yeah uh one of the things  about the series is like  the incredible production design and  it's just there's like as i've been  saying in other interviews but there's  like no weak  link in this chain from the  cinematography to the production design  to  everything i mean the sh i can't believe  it's not all we're talking about is just  how beautifully the show is shot  and how beautifully it's designed it's i  could just talk about that all day  um berlin was an incredible place for  locations but the production designer  just did an incredible julie just did  such a beautiful job as well  i mean you are transported back in time  and  we were able to film scenes  that were russia and mexico city and  vegas all in berlin  and it really just put all of us i mean  i recognize because i'm a director how  much work goes into the moment that an  actor arrives on  set and goes well look at this this is  cool and it's like oh it's taken a year  of work  to get to that moment where we get to  film that scene and for me it was like  a present every day because i would just  get to show up and be like cool where  are we going today and show up to these  beautifully decked  even scenes like going shopping in a  department store  like a 1950s department store and it was  just  the lushness of the design was so  beautiful it was wonderful  as i was watching it i was just you know  uh when you get to be transported like  that it's just uh  it adds so much and i think right now in  this moment in time this show is a  really fun  escape for that reason we're all locked  in our houses and to actually get to  just  watch this beautiful i mean it's such a  compelling story but it's also just a  beautifully shot and designed  show i know you have what the  constitution means to me which is coming  up for you  uh but i'm curious if there's any other  directing  projects uh that are coming up for you  besides that one  um i have a project called five women  that i've been developing but that we're  not gonna be able to film until the  world opens back  up you know we were really lucky with  what the constitution means to me that  we finished filming i think  a week before the shutdown happened and  um  so and projects like queen's gambit had  that  happen as well where we were done  filming and you can really do a lot of  post  editing and sound work during this  quarantine but it's a lot harder to  shoot  and i have a newborn so i'm not going  into production anytime soon but when i  do five women i'll be the first thing  that i do  got it on that note i need to stop i'm  just to say congrats on this project and  everything else  and uh and you know i'm sure i'll talk  to dormant soon  um yeah later have a good day great to  talk to you bye

